  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nWith high-amplitude ridging encompassing the western CONUS and a \ntrough axis nosing into the Mid-Atlantic, much of the Plains and \nMidwest sits beneath northwesterly flow aloft. A trough looming over \nBritish Columbia and the Pacific Northwest will march the ridge \neastward over the coming days. With the ridge nearing, expect warmer \nbut still seasonal temperatures in the mid to upper 80s today and \nFriday. Early Friday morning, far northeastern Kansas and \nnorthwestern Missouri could see some brief rain showers (15-30% \nchance). Storms are expected to initiate in southern South Dakota and \nnorthern Nebraska overnight with an enhancement of the low-level \njet. Some guidance has begun to depict an area of isentropic ascent \nin southeastern Nebraska in the early morning hours. With convection \nmoving generally to the southeast in this northwest flow regime, \nthis localized area of isentropic forcing may be just enough to \nsustain showers into the northern fringes of the CWA. A similar \nsetup on Saturday morning (just shifted slightly eastward) could \nlead to additional rain across northern Missouri.\n\nAs the PNW trough slides east and compresses the ridge, high \npressure will eventually be corralled into Oklahoma and Texas over \nthe weekend. The tight pressure gradient along the ridge apex will \nlead to strong southerly flow Saturday into early next week with \nsustained winds around 10-15 MPH and gusts around 20-25 MPH. Daytime \ncloud cover on Saturday may be able to abate some of the heat, \nespecially along and north of US-36. As such, maximum temperatures \nin the north are expected to generally remain in the mid/upper 80s \nagain on Saturday. Further south, where confidence in cloud cover is \nlesser, temperatures could break into the lower 90s. Even with cloud \ncover, Saturday will feel warmer in comparison to the end of the \nweek with increasing moisture advection which will bring heat index \nvalues into the mid 90s west of US-65. Increasing temperatures and \ndewpoints Sunday through at least midweek will lead to several days \nof temperatures in the 90s and maximum heat index values around 100F \nareawide. HeatRisk is expected to reach Major (Level 3 of 4) Sunday \nand continue for the majority of the area through Wednesday, further \nsignifying the potential for a late summer hazardous heat event. \nShower and storm chances will return just about nightly from Monday \nnight through Thursday morning. Showers/storms would likely be \nfocused along the nose of the low-level jet which is expected to \nremain mostly north of Kansas and Missouri...a slight southerly \nshift could bring these showers into our northern tier or two of \ncounties, so held onto about 20-30% 6-hourly PoPs during the \nevening/overnight/early morning periods Monday through Thursday in \nour far north.",
